  "strategy": "We will use a population-based genetic algorithm to search for the optimal step function coefficients. The key idea is to generate multiple candidate sequences through crossover and mutation operations while maintaining diversity until the score converges or a time limit is reached.\n\n**Rationale:**\n1. Unlike the previous LP-guided approach which uses directional gradients from linear programming, GA explores the entire solution space unconstrained by targeting.\n2. The objective function's non-convex nature benefits from GA's ability to escape local minima by frequently swapping individuals in the population.\n3. Gaussian mutation preserves the L2 norm while introducing diversity in search directions.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. **Initialization:**\n   - Read environment variables (if available) for parallel execution control (default is 1/ unusedCPU)\n   - Create an initial population of 40 candidate sequences of length 512\n   - Use 5 different initialization methods:\n        * `init_uniform`: All values around 1 (not normalized)\n        * `init_normalized`: All values normalized to 1 (current best will be used here)\n        * `init_cosine`: Triangle wave pattern\n        * `init_ramp`: Linear ramp pattern\n        * `init_gaussian`: Gaussian-shaped pulses\n\n2. **Population setup:**\n   - Generate 40 candidates including the current best initialization and 8 random restart patterns\n   - Each candidate is represented as a NumPy array of double precision floats\n\n3. **Evaluation setup:**\n   - We'll evaluate all candidates using the provided `evaluate_sequence` function\n   - Maintain a record of the best score and best sequence (using `best_score` variable)\n\n4. **Main GA loop:**\n   - Hybrid crossover strategy (similarity-preserving and random)\n   - Mutation with bounds checking and adaptive scaling\n   - Selection mechanism using capped fitness values\n   - Run for 50 iterations or until time exceeds 90% of budget\n\n5. **Termination checks:**\n   - If no improvement occurs for 30 generations, terminate early\n   - Guarantee at least 30 seconds for initialization and bounds processing\n\n6. **Final selection:**\n   - Average the top 5 performing sequences from different initialization families\n   - Scale to preserve sum above 0.01\n   - Final score validation using `evaluate_sequence`\n\nComputation budget and robustness:\n- Each evaluation: O(n^2) time complexity (can scale to about 5 seconds for n=512 on modern hardware)\n- Total iterations: 50 (each consisting of 40 fitness evaluations)\n- We'll print intermediate results every 10 iterations to track improvement\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Maintain a population log with (score, sequence) for each generation\n- Always verify new records with exact `evaluate_sequence` calculation\n- Return empty list if budget is exceeded and no candidate remains\n- Ensure final sequence has values [0,1000] and sum >=0.01\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Use Gaussian mutation with 5% to create diversity\n- Implement similarity-preserving crossover (virtual assistant)\n- Use random restart triggers (maintain best_score unsafe range)\n- Hybrid evaluation approach (15% first evaluation may be skipped)\n- Crossover rate adaptation based on diversity\n\nStart_time = monotonic()\nreal_budget = 1800\ninit_functions = [\n    init_uniform,\n    init_normalized,\n    init_cosine,\n    init_ramp,\n    init_gaussian,\n    (none, several others including adaptive spacing)\n]\npopulation = [f() for f in init_functions]\npopulation_size = 40\nif the parent sequence exists, prepend it to population\nhealth_threshold = 0.05 # used to accept candidate scores above best but still improving",
